{"availability":"available","full":"Neutrinic/flare","name":"flare","description":"Full-stack OpenTelemetry observability for Apache Spark","category":"Developer tools","language":"Scala","stars":20,"forks":2,"created":"2026-02-27T01:25:21Z","updated":"2026-10-07T06:22:52Z","license":"Apache-2.0","branch":"main","homepage":"https://neutrinic.github.io/flare/","topics":["apache-spark","opentelemetry","otel","spark"],"demo":"https://neutrinic.github.io/flare/","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T08:56:22.293Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-09T08:56:22.293Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-09T08:56:22.293Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-07T02:54:26.013Z","stars":20,"forks":2},{"at":"2026-10-09T08:56:22.293Z","stars":20,"forks":2}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Full-stack OpenTelemetry observability for Apache Spark: traces, metrics and logs correlated across driver and executor JVMs.","Most Spark observability stops at the driver. You get a stage with an aggregate duration, but not which executor ran slow, which partition was skewed, or where a retry happened.","Flare is an extension to the OpenTelemetry Java agent. On the driver it gives every stage its own W3C traceparent and passes it to the tasks through Spark's task properties. On the executor it restores that context, so each task becomes a real span, timed on the executor, under its own stage. The full hierarchy, application → sql → job → stage → task, spans the driver and executor JVMs, with metrics and logs linked to it. Everything is exported over OTLP to any backend."],"features":["Traces: the full span hierarchy across driver and executor JVMs, with SQL plans, failures and","task metrics as attributes. Granularity, slow-task and retry-only filters, and sampling by application.","Metrics: task duration histograms with exemplar links to their traces, shuffle and stage","totals, and cluster lifecycle metrics.","Logs: driver and executor logs linked to the 